  <section>
    <h2>Why sending photos matters more than describing the phone</h2>
    <p>Photos capture things descriptions miss. Common examples:</p>
    <p>'Small scratches on the screen' — actually visible only in specific lighting, or actually significant enough to affect touch sensitivity. Photo shows which one.</p>
    <p>'Some wear on the back' — actually a dent, a chip, or minor cosmetic scuffing. Very different impacts on the quote.</p>
    <p>'Screen crack in the corner' — actually a small crack, a spider-web pattern, or a full break with touch failing. Very different implications for buyback.</p>
    <p>'Basically new' — actually shows visible wear on the frame or back that the seller has stopped noticing.</p>
    <p>Three photos (front, back, About screen) plus close-ups of any damage covers almost everything the recycler needs to give an accurate estimate. Verbal descriptions alone almost always lead to bigger gaps between estimate and confirmed quote at inspection.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Battery health — the underappreciated variable</h2>
    <p>Battery health matters more than most sellers realise. On iPhone specifically, Settings → Battery → Battery Health shows a Maximum Capacity percentage. This is a first-order input to the buyback quote:</p>
    <p>Maximum Capacity 90%+ — phone functions like new; buyback quote reflects that.</p>
    <p>Maximum Capacity 80-89% — noticeable battery drain in daily use; buyback quote reduces to account for eventual replacement cost.</p>
    <p>Maximum Capacity below 80% — phone shows 'Service' notification and battery is past its ideal replacement threshold; buyback quote reduces more.</p>
    <p>For Android, there's no equivalent per-percentage indicator built-in, but general battery-drain experience from daily use is informative. If the phone goes from 100% to 50% in a couple of hours, mention it — the recycler needs to know.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Cracks, water damage, and other reduction reasons</h2>
    <p>Cracked screens: still often qualify for a reduced quote if the phone works. The reduction accounts for the cost of a replacement screen in the local second-hand market. Some cracks are cosmetic (rear glass on iPhone); others are functional (touch input failing). Flag which type in your photo.</p>
    <p>Bent frames: usually a significant reduction — often means the phone was dropped hard and internal components may be misaligned.</p>
    <p>Water damage indicators: iPhones and modern Androids have a small water indicator visible when a SIM tray is removed — if it's turned pink or red, the phone has been exposed to liquid. This significantly affects (usually eliminates) buyback qualification.</p>
    <p>Dead spots on screen or touch: often reduces quote significantly because second-hand buyers can't easily verify how bad the issue is.</p>
    <p>Missing SIM tray, damaged charging port, dead speakers: each reduces the quote proportional to repair cost.</p>
  </section>

